					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jacob-conwell" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1372582); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1372582&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200716" alt="Jacob Conwell" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1372582)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jacob-conwell" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1372582); return false;" >Jacob Conwell</a></span> <span class="odate time_1532296891 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Jul 2018 22:01</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3881890">
									

<p>I'm torn.</p>
<p>On one hand, there is heart to this article. Lines like &quot;I'm sorry, bud, but how do you expect the past to stay away from you when you won't stop living in it?&quot; were particularly effective at bringing some emotion to the table.</p>
<p>On the other hand, the rest of the article is just so dry. You have a device that allows you to look into the origin of any object or person, with a witty and fun narrator to boot. The possibility there for fun examples, test logs, etc are exciting. But then you don't really do anything with it. Instead, the majority of the log on the researcher's descent is rather boring and riddled with cliches (Lover eaten by another SCP object, obsession over the loss of said lover, the inability of the Foundation to monitor and log what they are having a researcher do with an incredibly powerful piece of machinery and no follow up on this person's mental status during these tests, etc.)</p>
<p>Going to no vote. Interesting premise, but profoundly lacking on the execution.</p>

